-- title:
--   Blocks are finite sets
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$, let $\ell \in \mathbb{R}$ with $\ell \ge 0$, and let $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$. The *block* of side $\ell$ at $x$ (Definition 5.10) is the set of lattice points inside the closed cube of side $\ell$ centred at $x$:
--   $$Q_\ell(x) \;=\; \mathbb{Z}^d \cap \bar A_\ell(x), \qquad \bar A_\ell(x) = \Bigl\{y \in \mathbb{R}^d : \lVert y - x\rVert_\infty \le \tfrac{\ell}{2}\Bigr\}, \qquad \lVert u \rVert_\infty = \sup_{i} |u_i| .$$
--   It is the renormalization step of Section 5.2, in which the lattice points sitting in a cube of side $\ell$ are collected into a single coarse vertex. Then
--
--   $$Q_\ell(x) \text{ is a finite set.}$$
--
--   Finiteness is the property that makes a block usable as a combinatorial object: cardinalities of blocks can be counted, maxima over a block are attained, and the multiplicity bookkeeping of the assembly argument — which counts how many pairs are handled at each scale and centre — is meaningful.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Only $\ell \ge 0$ is assumed; no lower bound such as $\ell \ge 1$ is needed for finiteness, and in particular the empty block is allowed.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Renormalization.lean#L141-L145

